---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Security & Vulnerabilities - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="testing" subChapterId="security-and-vulnerabilities">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI-generated code tends to take the shortest path to a working feature. That path often skips security considerations unless you explicitly ask for
				them. The AI is like a new grad focused on making the tests pass: it will produce functional code, but it will not think about who might
				try to abuse it.
			</p>

			<h3>Assume it is Insecure</h3>

			<p>
				Unless your prompt mentions specific security requirements, the AI will generally not add them. Input validation, SQL parameterization, CSRF tokens, rate limiting,
				authentication checks on new endpoints: these are the kinds of things that get skipped when the AI is focused on making a feature work.
			</p>

			<p>
				This is not unique to AI. Human developers skip these things too. The difference is that AI produces code
				faster, and is more reliant on code review to catch issues.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Input validation</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						AI often trusts user input directly. Look for missing validation on form fields, API parameters, and file uploads. Check that values are
						sanitized before being used in queries, rendered in HTML, or passed to external services.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Authentication and authorization</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						New endpoints or pages may not have auth checks. The AI adds the route and the handler but may forget to verify that the user is logged in or
						has permission to access that resource.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Secrets and credentials</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Watch for API keys or connection strings hardcoded directly in the source. The AI may add a placeholder value that looks like it should be an
						environment variable but is actually committed to the repo.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Dependency choices</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						When the AI installs packages, it may choose outdated or unmaintained libraries with known vulnerabilities. Check that dependencies are
						current and actively maintained.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Make Security Part of Your Prompts</h3>

			<p>
				The most reliable way to get secure code from the AI is to mention it in your prompt or in your{" "}
				<a href="/task-breakdowns/agents-md" class="text-teal-600 underline">AGENTS.md</a> file. If your project-level instructions say "validate
				user input with zod" and "use environment variables for secrets" the AI will follow those rules more consistently than if you rely on remembering to ask each time.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example AGENTS.md entry</div>
				<ul class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
					<li>All user input must be validated and sanitized with zod, see /src/x/y/z.ts for an example implementation</li>
					<li>Never hardcode API keys or secrets</li>
					<li>All new API endpoints must include authentication and authorization checks</li>
					<li>Use parameterized queries for all database operations, see /src/x/y/z.ts for an example implementation</li>
				</ul>
			</div>

			<p>
				Even with these instructions in place, the AI will not catch everything. It follows rules mechanically, and security often requires contextual
				judgment that the AI lacks.
			</p>

			<h3>Security Review as a Habit</h3>

			<p>
				Build security review into your normal code review process, not as a separate step that happens later. Every time you review AI-generated code, scan
				for the common issues: unsanitized input, missing auth, hardcoded values, overly verbose logs, and unsafe defaults.
			</p>

			<p>
				You can also ask the AI itself to do a security review of its own output. This is not a substitute for human review, but it catches some issues.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example prompt</div>
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					Review the code on this branch for security vulnerabilities. List any issues you find and we can discuss them.
				</p>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Keep in mind">
				<p>
					The AI is eager and takes the easy route. It will not push back on insecure patterns unless you tell it to look for them. Security is your
					responsibility, not the AI's. Use the AI as a tool to help catch issues, but do not assume it has covered everything.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
